  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nClear skies and light winds headed into Friday morning. This will\nlead to another chilly morning, with lows in the lower-to-mid 30s\nacross much of Southern New Mexico and Far West Texas. Guidance\nkeeps the El Paso metro just above the freezing mark, though I\nwouldn't be surprised to see portions of Southwest New Mexico and\nthe rural Rio Grande Valley freeze for a few hours Friday \nmorning.\n\nFriday will be warmer, pushing afternoon highs to the mid-to-\nupper 60s. This is due to increasing heights aloft ahead of a\nstrong Pacific high and a light west surface flow. Most of the \nday will be sunny, with a few high clouds spilling down from the \nFour Corners during the evening and overnight hours.\n\n80-knot 500mb jet streak over the Four Corners on Saturday will \nmove down the front of the ridge aloft, crossing over Northern \nNew Mexico. 995mb lee surface low formation across the Texas\nPanhandle will allow for breezy conditions locally Saturday \nafternoon. Not super impressed with the surface pressure gradient\nthis far south, so west winds 15 to 25 mph with peak gusts to 35\nmph is currently the most likely outcome for the I-10/I-25\ncorridors. Will need to monitor for stronger gusts (40-50 mph) \nover the Sacramento Mountains, which are the only zones relatively\nclose to Wind Advisory criteria.\n\nBackdoor cold front behind this system will knock temperatures \nback down to normal (lowland highs lower 60s) on Sunday. This \nlooks to be a brief cool down and quick rebound headed into next \nweek with dry and zonal flow over the region Monday/Tuesday.\n\nGFS and ECMWF deterministic solutions are in good agreement \nresolving a deeper upper low over the Southwestern U.S. sometime \nnext week (December 2 to 4). This would be our next shot at \nprecipitation chances. Right now, forecast favors Wednesday with\nwidespread showers across Western New Mexico. This would be \nlowland rain and mountain snow above 7000 feet AGL.",
